    "result_text": "I implemented `complete_paragraph` in `app/tgi.py`:\n\n```python\nclient = InferenceClient(model=endpoint_url)\ngenerated = client.text_generation(\n    prompt,\n    max_new_tokens=200,\n    stop=[\"\\n\\n\"],\n    return_full_text=False,\n)\nreturn generated.rstrip()\n```\n\n- **Stop setting:** The pinned huggingface-hub 2.0.0 names the stop parameter `stop`, not the older `stop_sequences`. I checked this in the installed source.\n- **Prompt excluded:** `return_full_text=False` is set explicitly so the prompt is never included in the output, whatever the server's default is.\n- **Trailing whitespace:** TGI keeps the matched `\"\\n\\n\"` at the end of the generated text. `rstrip()` removes it along with any other trailing whitespace. Leading whitespace is left alone, since you only asked for trailing whitespace to be removed.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes, but the only test there just checks that the module imports. To check the request itself, I faked the network call. The function sent `{\"inputs\": \"Once upon\", \"parameters\": {\"max_new_tokens\": 200, \"return_full_text\": false, \"stop\": [\"\\n\\n\"]}}` to the endpoint URL. It turned a reply of `\" A paragraph.  \\n\\n\"` into `\" A paragraph.\"`. I haven't run it against a real TGI server.\n\n**One thing to clean up:** my permissions didn't allow deleting the scratch script I used for that check. Please delete `_check_tgi.py` in the project root."
